<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9961"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Iq<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Iq<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 01, 2005 - 1:49 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sheriff Byron Motter of Harvey County says his office has been in contact with Wichita police over their only outstanding cold case, that of 46-year-old Agnes Hayes, who was found strangled under a bridge near the town of Sedgwick in 2001. Motter says he hasn’t asked for a new DNA analysis, but he’s watching Wichita carefully, hoping new evidence may link Hayes’ murder to BTK as well.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
